[Nr] (An empty null index used to mark the beginning)
.interp (Points to the OS program loader)
.note.gnu.property (Controls CPU-specific security features)
.note.gnu.build-id (A unique signature for crash tracking)
.note.ABI-tag (Tells the CPU what version of Linux this code needs)
.gnu.hash (Speeds up finding library functions)
.dynsym (The list of external system symbols, like printf)
.dynstr (The text names of those external functions)
.gnu.version (Tracks version numbers of system libraries)
.gnu.version_r (Lists library requirements for the OS)
.rela.dyn (Addresses to rewrite when the program loads into memory)
.rela.plt (Specific link addresses for jumping to system code)
.init (Setup code that fires before your code starts)
.plt (Procedure Linkage Table - the gateway to external libraries)
.plt.got (Extra helper table for standard library connections)
.text 👈 (Your actual assembly code logic lives here!)
.fini (Cleanup code that runs when the program shuts down)
.rodata (Read-only constants, like fixed text strings)
.eh_frame_hdr (Tells the OS how to handle fatal errors safely)
.eh_frame (The blueprint for unwinding the stack if a crash happens)
.init_array (A list of boot-up pointers used by libraries)
.fini_array (A list of shutdown pointers used by libraries)
.dynamic (Tells the system how to dynamically link libraries)
.got (Global Offset Table - stores addresses of external variables)
.got.plt (Global Offset Table specific to running active functions)
.data 👈 (Your pre-defined variables live here!)
.bss 👈 (Your empty, reserved memory buffers live here!)
.comment (Version notes from the compiler)
.symtab (The Symbol Table - a master map connecting memory addresses to code names)
.strtab (The String Table - stores raw text of names inside the code)
.shstrtab (Section Header String Table - holds the literal text labels like .text or .data)